[s0l0!-header][h1]Accessing Union Members[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

You access the members of a union variable by using the [b]. dot operator [/b]between the variable name and the member name. 
When assignment is performed, the union memory location will be used for that member until another member assignment is performed. 

Trying to access a member that isn't occupying the memory location gives [b]unexpected results[/b].

The following program demonstrates accessing union members:[code format="c" codeId="2109"]union val {
  int int_num;
  float fl_num;
  char str[20]; 
};
  
union val test;

test.int_num = 123;
test.fl_num = 98.76;
strcpy(test.str, "hello");

printf("%d\n", test.int_num);
printf("%f\n", test.fl_num);
printf("%s\n", test.str);[/code]
The last assignment overrides previous assignments, which is why [b]str[/b] stores a value and accessing [b]int_num[/b] and [b]fl_num[/b] is meaningless.
[note]Run the code and see how it works![/note]

