[s0l0!-header][h1]Structures With Unions[/h1][/s0l0!-header] 

Unions are often used within structures because a structure can have a member to keep track of which union member stores a value. 
For example, in the following program, a vehicle struct uses either a vehicle identification number (VIN) or an assigned id, but not both:[code format="c" codeId="2110"]typedef struct {
  char make[20];
  int model_year;
  int id_type; /* 0 for id_num, 1 for VIN */
[b]  union {
    int id_num;
    char VIN[20]; 
  } id;[/b]
} vehicle;

vehicle car1;
strcpy(car1.make, "Ford");
car1.model_year = 2017;
car1.id_type = 0;
[b]car1.id.id_num = 123098; [/b][/code]
Note that the union was declared inside the structure. When doing this, a [b]union name[/b] was required at the end of the declaration. 
A union with a union tag could have been declared outside the structure, but with such a specific use, the union within the struct provides easier to understand the code.

Note also the [b]dot operator[/b] is used twice to access union members of struct members.

The id_type keeps track of which union member stores a value. The following statements display car1 data, using the id_type to determine which union member to read:[code format="c" codeId="2111"]/* display vehicle data */
printf("Make: %s\n", car1.make);
printf("Model Year: %d\n", car1.model_year);
if (car1.id_type == 0)
  printf("ID: %d\n", car1.id.id_num);
else
  printf("ID: %s\n", car1.id.VIN); [/code]
[note]A union can also contain a structure.[/note]

